#N canvas 0 0 700 500 10;
#X obj 20 20 inlet;
#X text 110 20 one sounding voice \, wrapping a play_loop~ pointed at a loadable array. inlet accepts bang (stop: 5ms fade to silence) \, "gain <0..1ish>" (per-trigger fade-in target for the NEXT play \, default 1.0 \, does not affect an already-sounding voice) \, "pitch <semitones>" (multiplies the play list's rate by pow(2 \, semitones/12) at the NEXT play trigger \, default 0 semitones = 1x \, composes with rate's own sign so reverse + pitch-shifted-reverse both work) \, "mode <loop|shot>" (default loop = current continuous-wrap behavior \, shot = plays through once then auto-stops -- takes effect at the NEXT play trigger) \, or a 4-element list "rate startSamples endSamples arrayName" (play: re-points play_loop~ at arrayName \, sets its loop params \, retriggers from loop start \, and 5ms-fades in to the current stored gain target). creation arg: default array name to point at before the first real message arrives (callers should always send a real one before expecting sound).;
#X obj 20 60 route bang gain pitch mode;
#X msg 20 100 0 5;
#X obj 150 100 unpack f f f s;
#X obj 300 140 symbol;
#X msg 300 180 set \$1;
#X obj 150 180 pack f f f;
#X obj 150 220 t b b l;
#X obj 150 260 pack f 5;
#X obj 20 300 line~;
#X obj 20 340 play_loop~ \$1;
#X obj 20 380 *~;
#X obj 20 420 outlet~;
#X text 300 220 turn 10 (2026-09-18): pack here is float-only (rate/end/start) \, so plain numeric creation args are fine -- the "arrayname" element travels on its OWN branch (unpack outlet3 -> symbol -> msg set \$1) straight into play_loop~ \, never through pack. An earlier probe found that mixing a symbol into a [pack f f f <name>] creation arg is invalid syntax -- Pd interprets an unrecognized word as a pointer-type inlet \, not a symbol default \, and errors "consistency check failed: gpointer_copy" the first time a real symbol arrives. The fix \, if a symbol slot in a pack is ever needed here later \, is the literal type keyword 's' \, e.g. [pack f f f s] -- never a sample value.;
#X obj 20 460 float 1;
#X text 150 460 turn 20 (2026-09-18 \, per-voice sample parameters step 3): "gain" is a per-trigger fade-in target \, not a live/continuous volume control -- sending it only updates the stored value (cold inlet) \, it does not retarget an already-sounding voice's line~. Deliberately scoped this way: this abstraction's envelope is a play/stop trigger envelope \, not a channel fader \; a live-ramped continuous volume belongs to mixer.pd's per-voice vol/send (once file_poly.pd is wired into it -- see the mixer AUX bus TODO) \, not duplicated here. [float 1]'s creation arg was probe-verified to set its initial stored value immediately (no loadbang/prior-set needed) before this was relied on. route's arity grew from "bang" (2 outlets) to "bang gain" (3 outlets) \, which moved the reject/play-list outlet from index 1 to index 2 -- the one existing connection that referenced the old index 1 was updated deliberately \, not a gotcha #7 accident. The old static [msg 1 5] was repurposed in place into [pack f 5] (same file line \, same index 9) rather than left as a dead orphan. Hit a NEW variant of gotcha #7 while doing this: connect lines are processed sequentially as the file loads \, so a connect referencing a newly-appended object must appear AFTER that object's own #X line in the file \, not merely after computing the right index -- an object appended after the existing connect block \, referenced by an edited connect line still sitting inside that earlier block \, failed with "cannot connect to non-existing object" until the object was moved to before all connects (matching every other file in this codebase's own convention of objects-then-connects).;
#X obj 300 260 semitones_to_rate;
#X obj 150 300 * 1;
#X text 300 300 turn 21 (2026-09-18 \, per-voice sample parameters wiring): rate*pitch composition lives here \, not in file_poly.pd or mapping.pd \, because this is the abstraction that owns one voice's full parameter set (same reasoning gain already followed). "pitch" route-matches to [semitones_to_rate] \, whose output feeds [* 1]'s COLD inlet directly -- no separate store-plus-bang needed here (unlike gain) because rate's own per-trigger arrival on unpack's outlet0 already fires the multiply's HOT inlet every play \, right-to-left firing (gotcha #4) still lands it last relative to end/start same as the old direct wire did. [* 1]'s creation arg was probe-verified to seed its cold inlet to 1.0 (no pitch shift) before any "pitch" message ever arrives \, same discipline as [float 1] before it.;
#X obj 500 260 route loop shot;
#X obj 500 300 float 0;
#X msg 560 300 0;
#X msg 620 300 1;
#X obj 500 340 pack f f f;
#X obj 500 380 expr abs(\$f3-\$f2)/abs(\$f1);
#X obj 660 340 loadbang;
#X obj 660 380 samplerate~;
#X obj 500 420 pack f f;
#X obj 500 460 expr \$f1/\$f2*1000;
#X obj 500 500 float 0;
#X obj 500 540 sel 0 1;
#X msg 560 540 stop;
#X obj 500 580 delay;
#X text 660 420 turn 22 (2026-09-18 \, mode shot): duration-timer design \, not a signal-domain phase-wrap detector -- probed [expr]'s list-auto-distribution across \$f1/\$f2/\$f3 first (a single list on its leftmost inlet correctly fans out to every numbered variable \, confirmed against known values before relying on it) specifically to avoid the multi-step hot/cold ordering headaches that a chain of separate [-]/[abs]/[/] objects would have needed: unpack's rate outlet fires LAST (gotcha #4) \, so [pack f f f] (rate hot \, start+end cold) always emits a correctly-ordered "rate start end" list exactly when rate arrives \, and [expr abs(\$f3-\$f2)/abs(\$f1)] computes duration-in-samples \, both direction-signs (abs()'d) \, in one step. Second pack+expr converts to ms via a dedicated [loadbang]->[samplerate~] query (this abstraction's own \, separate from play_loop~.pd's internal one -- gotcha #8: query the real fact \, don't assume). "mode" route-matches "loop"/"shot" into a flag store (0/1) \; at the SAME play-trigger fan-out point gain's stored value is read (t b b l outlet 0) \, the flag is banged into [sel 0 1]: loop sends "stop" to [delay] (cancels a stale timer left over from a previous shot-mode play of this same voice) \; shot bangs the stored duration-ms value into [delay]'s inlet (sets + starts). [delay]'s "stop" message was probe-verified to actually cancel a pending timer (not just a no-op) before this was relied on. When the timer fires \, its bang feeds the SAME msg "0 5" the external bang-to-stop message already uses -- one fade-out mechanism \, two ways to trigger it. Known minor (non-audible) inefficiency \, not fixed: an early manual stop during a shot-mode play doesn't cancel that play's pending timer \, so msg "0 5" fires twice (once from the manual stop \, once later from the stale timer) -- harmless since re-sending the same fade target to line~ is idempotent. Also: caught a real gotcha #7 recurrence WHILE BUILDING this -- these objects were first inserted BEFORE semitones_to_rate/[* 1]/turn21-text instead of after \, shifting those three from indices 17/18/19 to 33/34/35 while the existing connects still pointed at 17/18 \, silently rewiring unpack's rate output and semitones_to_rate's output into the WRONG new objects. Caught by re-reading the file's own index list right after the edit (not by a load error -- it would have loaded "clean" and been silently wrong) \, fixed by moving this whole block to come after those three objects instead \, restoring their original indices.;
